-- title:
--   The Section 8 ledger provides a pre-mesh placed-selector deficit constant
-- statement:
--   Write $L_n=\log n$, $s_n=n/\log n$, $y_n=\lfloor n^{2/9}\rfloor$, and $\mathcal P_{n,W}=\{p\text{ prime}:W<p\le y_n\}$. Fix head patterns, physical intervals, a guard ledger, $c>0$, $W\ge2$, $W\ge2d+1$, $0<\delta_*<1/18$, $\beta_a>0$, and $\mu>0$, with all head primes at most $W$. Suppose total families $\log Y,\Lambda_0,m_f,\widetilde q$ satisfy the Section 8 ledger $q_{\rm raw}-\widetilde q=o(s_n)$ and $A_0=O(s_n)$. Then a nonnegative constant $C_{\rm init}$ exists before the final mesh such that eventually every compatible fresh canonical bridge and source package satisfies
--   $$|\operatorname{SelectorValuationDeficit}(p)|\le C_{\rm init}\frac{q}{p\log n}\qquad(p\in\mathcal P_{n,W}).$$
--   Here compatibility means the prescribed protected and active coefficients and canonical guarded sample, exact synchronization of $\widetilde q$ with the guarded smooth-base mass, balanced and total coefficients in $[0,1]$, a nonempty broad smooth correction pool, and exact equality of the local height correction $d$ and active mass $q$ with the displayed Section 8 families. The eventual threshold is common to all meshes and such local choices.
